{{define "landing"}}<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>Peyva Sandbox</title>
<link rel="icon" href="data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg viewBox='0 0 24 24'%3E%3Crect width='24' height='24' rx='6' fill='%233b6ef6'/%3E%3Ctext x='12' y='17' font-family='Arial,sans-serif' font-size='14' font-weight='700' fill='white' text-anchor='middle'%3EP%3C/text%3E%3C/svg%3E">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="{{.AssetPrefix}}styles.css?v={{.StyleVersion}}">
</head>
<body data-chapter-slug="index">
<button type="button" class="sidebar-toggle" data-sidebar-toggle aria-label="Toggle chapter menu">
  <svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><line x1="3" y1="6" x2="21" y2="6"></line><line x1="3" y1="12" x2="21" y2="12"></line><line x1="3" y1="18" x2="21" y2="18"></line></svg>
</button>
<div class="sidebar-backdrop" data-sidebar-backdrop></div>
<div class="layout">
{{template "sidebar" .}}
<main class="content">
  <div class="chapter-header">
    <p class="chapter-eyebrow">Home</p>
    <h1>Peyva Sandbox</h1>
    <p class="chapter-subtitle">Learn distributed systems by building one. A wallet where alice sends bob $20, and the money arrives exactly once.</p>
  </div>

  <figure class="hero-panel">
    <a class="hero-link" href="{{.AssetPrefix}}images/chapter-0.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="{{.AssetPrefix}}images/chapter-0.webp" alt="Alice sends Bob $20: the story, and what is really happening underneath."></a>
    <figcaption>Alice sends Bob $20. That is the whole system, and the whole book. <a class="hero-expand-hint" href="{{.AssetPrefix}}images/chapter-0.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Click to view full size</a></figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="section-grid">
    <section class="block">
      <h2>1. What</h2>
      <ul class="why-list">
        <li>You build peyva, a small payments system, one piece per chapter.</li>
        <li>Each piece is added because the chapter before it showed you a failure. Nothing is there for its own sake.</li>
        <li>By the end: several copies behind a proxy, a store with a follower and a lease, a queue, an outbox, sharding, and a check that the money still adds up.</li>
        <li>There is no finished code to download. You prompt for it, run it, and see for yourself.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="block">
      <h2>2. Why</h2>
      <ul class="why-list">
        <li>Money is the one kind of data where every distributed systems mistake is visible and costs something.</li>
        <li>Reading about a lost update is forgettable. Watching alice lose 60 on your own machine is not.</li>
        <li>Five rules never break: money is never created or lost, never negative, never paid twice, only one component changes it, every move is written down. Every chapter tries to break one.</li>
        <li>Your assistant writes the code. You decide what it must do and check that it did. That is the skill.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="block">
      <h2>3. How</h2>
      <p class="lead-line">How a chapter works, in three steps.</p>
      <ul class="why-list">
        <li><b>Read</b> the What and the Why. About a hundred words each.</li>
        <li><b>Copy</b> each prompt into your own AI coding assistant. It writes the code, in the language you chose.</li>
        <li><b>Run it</b> and watch it fail the way the chapter said it would. Then fix it.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="lead-line">You need an AI coding assistant, one language, and one laptop. Chapter 0 asks for the language and your operating system, then hands you three files to save.</p>
      <p><a class="landing-start" href="{{.AssetPrefix}}chapter-0.html">Start with Chapter 0</a></p>
    </section>
  </div>
</main>
</div>
<script src="{{.AssetPrefix}}app.js?v={{.ScriptVersion}}"></script>
</body>
</html>
{{end}}
